(* mean over surviving links only. outage = P[SNR < 50 dB] including lost links;
   lost = fraction with zero received power, every ray outside the FoV)

Decomposition [dB]; positive = SNR lost
model     activity  tilt effect  pose-blind  outage +
cosine    sitting         -5.75        0.00      2.5%
cosine    walking         -4.92        0.00      1.7%
specular  sitting         -5.21        0.00      3.0%
specular  walking         -4.34        0.00      2.1%

  tilt effect = face-up -> genie. NEGATIVE means tilting HELPS: the
                IRS panels sit on the walls at z = 1.5 m, so a face-up
                PD is badly aimed for wall reflections. Mean SNR rises,
                but see the outage column -- the spread widens sharply.
  pose-blind  = genie -> blind, the cost of the controller not knowing
                the device pose, i.e. what assuming a face-up PD spends.

  NOTE pose-blind is exactly 0.00 dB under BOTH models. This is
  structural, not a rounding artefact. The cosine optimum n = u_out and
  the specular optimum bisector(u_in, u_out) are both built from
  POSITIONS alone; neither contains the detector normal. Device
  orientation therefore cannot change the optimal steering under either
  formulation, and knowing the pose is worth precisely nothing to a
  controller whose only decision is the element normal. Orientation-aware
  IRS control is not merely unimplemented but inexpressible in both
  models as formulated: pose can enter only through a further decision
  variable -- element activation, target assignment or source selection
  under a constraint -- that neither closed form carries.

  (An earlier version of this script reported a nonzero specular gap.
  That was an artefact: the serving-luminaire argmax ran over an array
  carrying cos_psi, which is zero outside the field of view, so the whole
  slice collapsed and np.argmax returned index 0. The pose-blind arm was
  charged for an arbitrary fallback index, not for lacking pose.)

